@startuml tls-acme
!include _style.iuml

title How certificates are issued and renewed (ACME HTTP-01)

participant "**edge-certbot**" as certbot #DCE9F7
participant "certbot/www\n(shared volume)" as webroot #EEF0F3
participant "certbot/conf\n= /etc/letsencrypt" as confdir #EEF0F3
participant "**edge-nginx**" as nginx #DCE9F7
participant "Let's Encrypt" as le #E7E0F5

note over certbot
  The container runs a loop, not a cron job:
  ""while :; do certbot renew; sleep 12h; done""
  ""certbot renew"" is a no-op until a cert is
  inside 30 days of expiry, so it is safe to
  run twice a day forever.
end note

autonumber "<b>[0]"

certbot -> le : renew request for track.caseyrquinn.com
activate le
le --> certbot : challenge token
deactivate le

certbot -> webroot : write token to\n/var/www/certbot/.well-known/acme-challenge/<token>
activate webroot

le -> nginx : GET http://track.caseyrquinn.com/.well-known/acme-challenge/<token>\n**over plain :80**
activate nginx
note right of nginx
  This is exactly why every :80 block carries

  ""location /.well-known/acme-challenge/ { root /var/www/certbot; }""

  **before** the catch-all 301. If that redirect came first,
  the challenge would bounce to HTTPS and renewal would fail —
  the classic way a proxy locks itself out of its own certs.
end note
nginx -> webroot : read token from the same volume
webroot --> nginx : token
deactivate webroot
nginx --> le : 200 + token
deactivate nginx

le -> le : token matches → domain control proven
le --> certbot : new fullchain.pem + privkey.pem
certbot -> confdir : write to /etc/letsencrypt/live/track.caseyrquinn.com/
activate confdir

note over nginx, confdir #FFF8E1
  **The renewal is on disk, but not yet in use.** nginx mounts
  ""certbot/conf"" read-only and reads certificates into memory at
  start / reload. certbot cannot signal it, so a renewed cert is
  **not** served until something reloads nginx.

  Relying on a push to ""main"" for that would mean a quiet repo
  serves an expired cert, so nginx reloads itself on a timer —
  see the next step.
end note
deactivate confdir

... up to 6h later ...

nginx -> nginx : ""nginx -s reload"" from its own 6h loop\n(""command:"" in docker-compose.yml)
activate nginx
nginx -> confdir : re-read certificates from disk
activate confdir
confdir --> nginx : renewed fullchain.pem + privkey.pem
deactivate confdir
nginx -> nginx : new workers serve the new cert,\nold workers finish in-flight requests
deactivate nginx

note over nginx #FFF8E1
  A reload is not a restart — no connection is dropped and
  the :443 listener is never released. Six hours is well
  inside the 30-day renewal window.

  See docs/ARCHITECTURE.md → "Why nginx reloads itself every six hours".
end note

@enduml